1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:04,700
Eu Tong Sen: The Eu Yan Sang
King Whose Name Is on the Street

2
00:00:04,700 --> 00:00:08,587
Every day, thousands of people
walk, drive or ride down Eu

3
00:00:08,667 --> 00:00:12,975
Tong Sen Street in Chinatown
without giving the name a thought.

4
00:00:12,975 --> 00:00:17,383
It belongs to a Penang-born tin
magnate who died in Hong Kong

5
00:00:17,463 --> 00:00:22,975
in 1941 — and unlike most of the men
whose names are on Singapore's streets,

6
00:00:23,055 --> 00:00:25,925
the business he built is still trading.

7
00:00:25,925 --> 00:00:30,091
Eu Yan Sang, the Chinese
medicine firm his father

8
00:00:30,171 --> 00:00:34,857
founded in a Malayan tin town
in 1879, marked its 147th

9
00:00:34,937 --> 00:00:40,229
anniversary in October 2026,
still headquartered in Singapore,

10
00:00:40,309 --> 00:00:43,775
still chaired by one of his descendants.

11
00:00:43,775 --> 00:00:48,294
This is the story of the man, the
empire behind the medicine shops,

12
00:00:48,374 --> 00:00:51,600
and the name that outlived
him in three cities.

13
00:00:51,600 --> 00:00:57,453
Eu Tong Sen's father, Eu Kong, left
Foshan in Guangdong for Penang in 1873,

14
00:00:57,533 --> 00:01:02,200
and his first ventures — textile
dyeing, a bakery — failed.

15
00:01:02,200 --> 00:01:06,677
He worked collecting debts for
a grocery shop, drifted into the

16
00:01:06,757 --> 00:01:11,305
tin country of Perak, and in 1879,
in the mining town of Gopeng,

17
00:01:11,385 --> 00:01:15,725
he opened a small shop selling
traditional Chinese medicine.

18
00:01:15,725 --> 00:01:20,250
Its customers were the Chinese
tin-mine workers of the Kinta Valley,

19
00:01:20,330 --> 00:01:22,350
many of them addicted to opium,

20
00:01:22,430 --> 00:01:26,819
and the name he chose was a promise:
Yan Sang, from the characters

21
00:01:26,899 --> 00:01:30,150
for benevolence and life
— "caring for mankind".

22
00:01:30,150 --> 00:01:34,091
The shop's own history, as
the company tells it today,

23
00:01:34,171 --> 00:01:37,000
begins and ends with that act of care.

24
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:40,065
The National Library
Board's account adds the

25
00:01:40,145 --> 00:01:43,150
harder edge the company
version leaves out.

26
00:01:43,150 --> 00:01:48,430
In 1882 Eu Kong obtained a general tax
farm for the Batang Padang district —

27
00:01:48,510 --> 00:01:52,169
a licence to collect the
government's taxes on opium,

28
00:01:52,249 --> 00:01:56,824
gambling, alcohol and pawnbroking
— and it was the farm's revenues

29
00:01:56,904 --> 00:02:01,700
that financed his tin mines and the
growth of the medicine business.

30
00:02:01,700 --> 00:02:05,193
The medicine shop for
opium addicts and the opium

31
00:02:05,273 --> 00:02:08,700
tax farm were two sides
of the same enterprise.

32
00:02:08,700 --> 00:02:11,350
Both belong in the story.

33
00:02:11,350 --> 00:02:14,747
Eu Tong Sen was born in Penang

34
00:02:14,827 --> 00:02:17,725
on the 23rd of July 1877.

35
00:02:17,725 --> 00:02:21,702
At four he was sent to Foshan
for a Chinese education —

36
00:02:21,782 --> 00:02:25,243
where he acquired the
lifelong love of Cantonese

37
00:02:25,323 --> 00:02:29,447
opera that later shaped his
Singapore building projects —

38
00:02:29,527 --> 00:02:32,625
and he was brought back to Malaya in 1892.

39
00:02:32,625 --> 00:02:36,865
His father had died in 1890,
and the business he finally

40
00:02:36,945 --> 00:02:40,954
took control of, on turning
21 in 1898, was depleted:

41
00:02:41,034 --> 00:02:46,354
the tax farms were being phased out,
the family mines were worked out,

42
00:02:46,434 --> 00:02:50,600
and his uncles had run down
Yan Sang's cash and stock.

43
00:02:50,600 --> 00:02:54,450
His answer was to start
again at the rock face.

44
00:02:54,450 --> 00:02:59,558
From 1898 to 1900 he prospected
for tin in the Kinta Valley,

45
00:02:59,638 --> 00:03:03,796
riding out daily on elephant
back with his uncle,

46
00:03:03,876 --> 00:03:07,075
and his first discovery came in 1900.

47
00:03:07,075 --> 00:03:11,785
By 1908 he owned 11 mines —
eight in Perak, two in Selangor,

48
00:03:11,865 --> 00:03:13,621
one in Negri Sembilan —

49
00:03:13,701 --> 00:03:19,050
employing some 12,000 workers, and
he was known as the King of Tin.

50
00:03:19,050 --> 00:03:25,094
He was among the first Chinese mine owners
to invest in Western hydraulic machinery,

51
00:03:25,174 --> 00:03:28,600
and he set up a foundry
and a smelting company.

52
00:03:28,600 --> 00:03:33,413
Rubber followed: some 15,000 acres
of plantations across Malaya,

53
00:03:33,493 --> 00:03:36,166
their estates named after his wives,

54
00:03:36,246 --> 00:03:39,075
daughters and other female relatives.

55
00:03:39,075 --> 00:03:42,732
The medicine shops were the
famous part of the empire,

56
00:03:42,812 --> 00:03:44,750
but not the most profitable.

57
00:03:44,750 --> 00:03:48,057
In 1906 Eu opened a
remittance office in Ipoh,

58
00:03:48,137 --> 00:03:53,138
sending miners' earnings home through
agents in Hong Kong and Canton;

59
00:03:53,218 --> 00:03:58,145
the volume grew from 33,000 Hong
Kong dollars in 1914 to more than 3

60
00:03:58,225 --> 00:04:02,268
million Hong Kong dollars in
1935, and by the late 1920s

61
00:04:02,348 --> 00:04:06,907
remittances were his most profitable
business, with Eu Yan Sang

62
00:04:06,987 --> 00:04:11,700
counted among the biggest remittance
agencies in Southeast Asia.

63
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:15,768
In 1920 he co-founded Lee
Wah Bank in Singapore,

64
00:04:15,848 --> 00:04:21,040
as its largest shareholder and
first chairman; his family ran

65
00:04:21,120 --> 00:04:25,700
it until it merged into
United Overseas Bank in 1973.

66
00:04:25,700 --> 00:04:29,575
The opium question followed
him through all of it.

67
00:04:29,575 --> 00:04:34,196
The National Library Board
records that in the early 1900s Eu

68
00:04:34,276 --> 00:04:39,360
held shares in opium farms in
Singapore and Penang while serving as

69
00:04:39,440 --> 00:04:44,061
vice-president of the Perak
Anti-Opium Society, and while his

70
00:04:44,141 --> 00:04:49,150
shops distributed a free anti-opium
cure in the campaign of 1906.

71
00:04:49,150 --> 00:04:53,669
His own justification, as recorded,
was the obligation he felt

72
00:04:53,749 --> 00:04:58,194
as a leading towkay; later
writers have read the shareholding

73
00:04:58,274 --> 00:05:02,650
as a way to control the trade
and price opium out of reach.

74
00:05:02,650 --> 00:05:07,525
The record carries both, and this
post leaves them side by side.

75
00:05:07,525 --> 00:05:12,054
Under Eu Tong Sen the medicine
chain spread beyond Perak —

76
00:05:12,134 --> 00:05:14,438
to Penang, Ipoh, Kuala Lumpur,

77
00:05:14,518 --> 00:05:18,650
and in 1909 to Hong Kong,
its first overseas branch.

78
00:05:18,650 --> 00:05:24,159
Singapore followed in 1910, at
265 to 271 South Bridge Road:

79
00:05:24,239 --> 00:05:28,910
a row of four shophouses
designed by Alfred Bidwell

80
00:05:28,990 --> 00:05:34,300
of Swan and Maclaren, the
architect of the Raffles Hotel.

81
00:05:34,300 --> 00:05:38,086
The Singapore branch worked
in three parts — remittance,

82
00:05:38,166 --> 00:05:41,677
wholesale Chinese herbs,
and pharmaceutical retail —

83
00:05:41,757 --> 00:05:45,336
and it was in this period
that Eu attached his family

84
00:05:45,416 --> 00:05:49,075
name to the shop's, making
Yan Sang into Eu Yan Sang.

85
00:05:49,075 --> 00:05:51,952
In 1910 he moved his headquarters,

86
00:05:52,032 --> 00:05:55,250
and himself, from Perak to Singapore.

87
00:05:55,250 --> 00:05:59,441
The street through Kreta Ayer
was then called Wayang Street,

88
00:05:59,521 --> 00:06:03,650
after the Chinese opera theatres
— wayang — that lined it.

89
00:06:03,650 --> 00:06:06,606
Eu took an interest in
it the way he took an

90
00:06:06,686 --> 00:06:10,275
interest in everything: he
bought property along it.

91
00:06:10,275 --> 00:06:14,502
In 1918 he improved part of
the street, adding a bridge,

92
00:06:14,582 --> 00:06:19,117
railings for the monsoon drains
and back lanes for theatres,

93
00:06:19,197 --> 00:06:22,347
and in 1919 the street was renamed Eu Tong

94
00:06:22,427 --> 00:06:26,350
Sen Street — in his lifetime,
when he was about 42.

95
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:29,717
The National Library Board
keeps two explanations

96
00:06:29,797 --> 00:06:32,400
for the honour, and both may be true.

97
00:06:32,400 --> 00:06:36,234
The first is his First World
War giving: he funded a tank

98
00:06:36,314 --> 00:06:39,873
and a scout fighter plane
for the British war effort,

99
00:06:39,953 --> 00:06:42,963
and the tank was painted
with "Chinese eyes",

100
00:06:43,043 --> 00:06:47,575
a marking later adopted by a
battalion of the Royal Tank Regiment.

101
00:06:47,575 --> 00:06:50,769
The second is the street
itself: he rebuilt it,

102
00:06:50,849 --> 00:06:54,809
and bought over its two opera
theatres, Heng Seng Peng and

103
00:06:54,889 --> 00:06:58,650
Heng Wai Sun, whose site is
now People's Park Complex.

104
00:06:58,650 --> 00:07:02,810
A third version circulates as
anecdote — that a theatre troupe

105
00:07:02,890 --> 00:07:07,393
offended his eighth wife, and Eu
bought up the street in response —

106
00:07:07,473 --> 00:07:11,850
and it is best left where it
belongs, as the story that is told.

107
00:07:11,850 --> 00:07:16,146
In 1927 his own theatre rose
on the street at number 80:

108
00:07:16,226 --> 00:07:19,349
the Majestic, built as Tien Yien Moh Toi,

109
00:07:19,429 --> 00:07:24,350
a Cantonese opera house with an
Art Deco façade of glazed tiles,

110
00:07:24,430 --> 00:07:29,275
blue dragons and opera figures,
designed by Swan and Maclaren.

111
00:07:29,275 --> 00:07:31,866
The official Chinatown trail tells why:

112
00:07:31,946 --> 00:07:35,838
he built it for his wife, after
she was reportedly refused

113
00:07:35,918 --> 00:07:39,000
admittance to an opera
performance elsewhere.

114
00:07:39,000 --> 00:07:42,868
The two theatres he had bought
were demolished long ago;

115
00:07:42,948 --> 00:07:48,015
the Majestic survives, conserved,
standing beside People's Park Complex —

116
00:07:48,095 --> 00:07:51,550
a cinema and then a mall
behind its tiled façade.

117
00:07:51,550 --> 00:07:55,437
At the junction with Upper
Cross Street stands the street's

118
00:07:55,517 --> 00:07:59,001
other 1927 landmark: the
six-storey Nam Tin Building,

119
00:07:59,081 --> 00:08:02,363
opened that February as
the Great Southern Hotel —

120
00:08:02,443 --> 00:08:06,801
then the tallest building in
Chinatown, the first Chinese hotel in

121
00:08:06,881 --> 00:08:11,050
Singapore with a lift, and
nicknamed the Raffles of Chinatown.

122
00:08:11,050 --> 00:08:13,520
Today it is the Yue Hwa Building,

123
00:08:13,600 --> 00:08:18,700
the Singapore flagship of the Hong
Kong Chinese-products emporium.

124
00:08:18,700 --> 00:08:20,825
Was it Eu's building?

125
00:08:20,825 --> 00:08:23,533
Here the record disagrees with itself,

126
00:08:23,613 --> 00:08:26,475
and the disagreement is worth printing.

127
00:08:26,475 --> 00:08:30,660
The National Library Board's
biography of Eu counts the Great

128
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:34,506
Southern Hotel among his
prominent Singapore buildings,

129
00:08:34,586 --> 00:08:39,900
and its BiblioAsia magazine calls the
Yue Hwa building one of his landmarks.

130
00:08:39,900 --> 00:08:43,805
But the Board's own history
of the hotel, and the national

131
00:08:43,885 --> 00:08:47,310
heritage portal Roots, say
the Nam Tin Building was

132
00:08:47,390 --> 00:08:51,100
owned by Lum Chang Holdings
and leased to its tenants.

133
00:08:51,100 --> 00:08:55,301
The Majestic's attribution to
Eu is solid; this one is not,

134
00:08:55,381 --> 00:09:00,025
and no honest version of his story
can claim the hotel outright.

135
00:09:00,025 --> 00:09:03,766
What is documented is the
building's later life:

136
00:09:03,846 --> 00:09:08,862
Lum Chang sold it in 1993 for
about 25 million Singapore dollars

137
00:09:08,942 --> 00:09:12,603
to the Yue Hwa group,
the hotel closed in 1994,

138
00:09:12,683 --> 00:09:16,186
and the rebuilt store
opened in October 1996,

139
00:09:16,266 --> 00:09:21,600
winning a national Architectural
Heritage Award the following year.

140
00:09:21,600 --> 00:09:24,825
Eu's giving reached beyond Chinatown.

141
00:09:24,825 --> 00:09:28,725
When Singapore raised
funds for Raffles College —

142
00:09:28,805 --> 00:09:33,923
the colony's centenary memorial,
opened in 1929 at Bukit Timah —

143
00:09:34,003 --> 00:09:37,578
he was among the business
leaders who pledged

144
00:09:37,658 --> 00:09:42,125
money from 1919, and his own
contribution was $100,000.

145
00:09:42,125 --> 00:09:46,052
The college named three of its
buildings for its great donors,

146
00:09:46,132 --> 00:09:50,060
and one of them, a student hostel
around the Upper Quadrangle,

147
00:09:50,140 --> 00:09:52,725
took his name: the Eu Tong Sen Building.

148
00:09:52,725 --> 00:09:57,451
The six college buildings were
gazetted together as a National

149
00:09:57,531 --> 00:10:01,946
Monument in 2009, and his
building served for years as the

150
00:10:02,026 --> 00:10:06,829
entrance of the National University
of Singapore's law faculty,

151
00:10:06,909 --> 00:10:11,250
before the faculty's move to
Kent Ridge in January 2026.

152
00:10:11,250 --> 00:10:15,720
In Hong Kong, his verified
gift was of the same kind:

153
00:10:15,800 --> 00:10:20,443
$30,000 for a gymnasium at
the University of Hong Kong,

154
00:10:20,523 --> 00:10:26,275
opened in 1936 and bearing his name
until the site was redeveloped.

155
00:10:26,275 --> 00:10:32,035
A larger figure — $55,000, dated
1918 — circulates in reference works,

156
00:10:32,115 --> 00:10:36,206
but no University of Hong
Kong source supports it,

157
00:10:36,286 --> 00:10:41,875
and the gymnasium is the gift the
university's own records confirm.

158
00:10:41,875 --> 00:10:46,466
In the late 1920s — the sources
give 1927, 1928 and 1930 —

159
00:10:46,546 --> 00:10:50,734
Eu moved his base to Hong
Kong, to run the remittance

160
00:10:50,814 --> 00:10:54,197
business more closely,
in poor health, and,

161
00:10:54,277 --> 00:11:00,075
the Board's account adds, with enemies
made by his political neutrality.

162
00:11:00,075 --> 00:11:04,626
He built three mansions there,
all called Yu Garden in Chinese:

163
00:11:04,706 --> 00:11:08,008
Eucliff at Repulse Bay,
Euston on Bonham Road,

164
00:11:08,088 --> 00:11:11,175
and Sirmio at Tai Po, the largest of them.

165
00:11:11,175 --> 00:11:15,188
The business he left increasingly
to senior managers and to

166
00:11:15,268 --> 00:11:19,500
his eldest son, Eu Keng Chee,
a Cambridge-trained accountant.

167
00:11:19,500 --> 00:11:22,999
On the 11th of May 1941, at Sirmio,

168
00:11:23,079 --> 00:11:27,475
Eu Tong Sen died of
heart failure, aged 63.

169
00:11:27,475 --> 00:11:31,503
He was buried in the grounds;
when the mansion was sold

170
00:11:31,583 --> 00:11:35,537
and demolished in the 1970s,
his descendants moved his

171
00:11:35,617 --> 00:11:39,725
remains to the Chinese
Permanent Cemetery at Tsuen Wan.

172
00:11:39,725 --> 00:11:42,700
He never returned to Singapore.

173
00:11:42,700 --> 00:11:46,800
One Hong Kong namesake
resists explanation.

174
00:11:46,800 --> 00:11:50,411
A village school at Shuen
Wan, in the same Tai Po

175
00:11:50,491 --> 00:11:53,726
district where Sirmio
stood, bore his name —

176
00:11:53,806 --> 00:11:58,250
the Shuen Wan Eu Tong Sen School
— until it closed in 1994.

177
00:11:58,250 --> 00:12:01,027
No source we could find, government,

178
00:12:01,107 --> 00:12:03,725
university or press, records why.

179
00:12:03,725 --> 00:12:08,300
Local accounts date the school
building to 1950, almost a decade

180
00:12:08,380 --> 00:12:12,810
after his death, which would
make the naming a memorial rather

181
00:12:12,890 --> 00:12:17,400
than a donor's gift; the documented
overlap is only geography.

182
00:12:17,400 --> 00:12:20,778
The site has since been
rebuilt as "Good House",

183
00:12:20,858 --> 00:12:27,117
a transitional housing project completed in
December 2024 — his name gone from the gate,

184
00:12:27,197 --> 00:12:31,375
the ground put to housing the
people the district now has.

185
00:12:31,375 --> 00:12:35,625
What returned to Singapore
was not the man but the firm.

186
00:12:35,625 --> 00:12:39,847
His estate was divided into 13
equal shares among his sons,

187
00:12:39,927 --> 00:12:44,660
a settlement that left no one in
control, and most of the empire —

188
00:12:44,740 --> 00:12:49,625
the mines, the estates, the theatres
— was sold off over the years.

189
00:12:49,625 --> 00:12:53,863
The medicine business split
in two: a Hong Kong firm,

190
00:12:53,943 --> 00:12:58,751
and a Singapore and Malaysia
firm, which incorporated in the

191
00:12:58,831 --> 00:13:03,475
1950s and listed on the
Singapore stock exchange in 1973.

192
00:13:03,475 --> 00:13:06,064
Lee Wah Bank, the other survivor,

193
00:13:06,144 --> 00:13:08,975
had merged into UOB that same year.

194
00:13:08,975 --> 00:13:13,125
The Singapore line's second
founder was his grandson.

195
00:13:13,125 --> 00:13:16,480
Richard Eu, fourth
generation of the family,

196
00:13:16,560 --> 00:13:21,320
was born in Hong Kong in 1947
and has lived in Singapore since

197
00:13:21,400 --> 00:13:25,458
he was two; a merchant banker
and venture capitalist,

198
00:13:25,538 --> 00:13:29,675
he joined the family firm
as general manager in 1989.

199
00:13:29,675 --> 00:13:34,517
In 1990 the Lum Chang group —
the same name that appears in the

200
00:13:34,597 --> 00:13:39,675
Nam Tin Building's history — had
taken over Eu Yan Sang Holdings.

201
00:13:39,675 --> 00:13:44,003
In 1993 Richard Eu led the
family's buyback of the medicine

202
00:13:44,083 --> 00:13:47,140
business for 21 million Singapore dollars,

203
00:13:47,220 --> 00:13:51,697
re-incorporated it in Singapore
as Eu Yan Sang International,

204
00:13:51,777 --> 00:13:55,806
folded the Hong Kong arm back
in during 1996 and 1997 —

205
00:13:55,886 --> 00:14:00,811
reuniting the two halves for the
first time in more than 50 years —

206
00:14:00,891 --> 00:14:05,000
and listed the group on the
SGX mainboard in July 2000.

207
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:09,024
The headquarters moved to
Tai Seng Drive in 2013;

208
00:14:09,104 --> 00:14:14,635
the old South Bridge Road building,
the group's home for a century,

209
00:14:14,715 --> 00:14:19,660
was sold in 2019 and reopened
in 2022 as a co-working space,

210
00:14:19,740 --> 00:14:23,425
its façade and its 1910
date still in place.

211
00:14:23,425 --> 00:14:27,176
The company's own milestones
tell its history more tidily

212
00:14:27,256 --> 00:14:31,007
than the record does — they
give Eu Kong's departure from

213
00:14:31,087 --> 00:14:34,839
Foshan a different year, hand
the business to Eu Tong Sen

214
00:14:34,919 --> 00:14:38,750
eight years early, and never
mention the tax farm at all.

215
00:14:38,750 --> 00:14:41,908
The business itself
has had a busier recent

216
00:14:41,988 --> 00:14:45,000
history than its heritage pages suggest.

217
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:49,596
In 2016 the group was taken
private, at a valuation of

218
00:14:49,676 --> 00:14:54,359
about 269 million Singapore
dollars, by a consortium of

219
00:14:54,439 --> 00:14:59,375
Tower Capital Asia, Temasek
and members of the Eu family.

220
00:14:59,375 --> 00:15:04,827
In June 2024 it changed hands
again: Japan's Rohto Pharmaceutical

221
00:15:04,907 --> 00:15:10,104
took about 60 per cent and Mitsui
about 30 per cent, in a deal

222
00:15:10,184 --> 00:15:15,375
valuing the company at roughly
808 million Singapore dollars.

223
00:15:15,375 --> 00:15:18,965
The Eu family's remaining
stake, about 10 per cent,

224
00:15:19,045 --> 00:15:22,707
was rolled over by Richard
Eu and his son Richie Eu,

225
00:15:22,787 --> 00:15:27,384
of the fifth generation — and
Richard Eu, the grandson who bought

226
00:15:27,464 --> 00:15:31,350
the business back in 1993,
remains its chairman today.

227
00:15:31,350 --> 00:15:36,407
The 2024 sale documents counted
more than 170 retail outlets

228
00:15:36,487 --> 00:15:41,629
and 30 traditional Chinese
medicine clinics across Singapore,

229
00:15:41,709 --> 00:15:46,675
Hong Kong and Malaysia, with
43 stores in Singapore alone.

230
00:15:46,675 --> 00:15:50,628
The shelves hold the old
standbys — bottled bird's nest,

231
00:15:50,708 --> 00:15:53,293
Bak Foong pills, essence of chicken —

232
00:15:53,373 --> 00:15:57,550
beside collagen, NMN supplements
and ready-to-drink soups.

233
00:15:57,550 --> 00:16:03,419
In October 2026 the company
marked its 147th anniversary,

234
00:16:03,499 --> 00:16:05,716
and its management was

235
00:16:05,796 --> 00:16:10,075
already talking about the 150th, in 2029.

236
00:16:10,075 --> 00:16:14,982
Where it fits in the bigger story: Eu
Tong Sen's mansions are demolished,

237
00:16:15,062 --> 00:16:18,261
his mines and estates
sold, his name carried now

238
00:16:18,341 --> 00:16:23,179
by a street most people hurry down,
a hostel block on a monument campus,

239
00:16:23,259 --> 00:16:26,675
and a Hong Kong school
sign that no longer exists.

240
00:16:26,675 --> 00:16:29,782
But the smallest thing his family built —

241
00:16:29,862 --> 00:16:35,145
one medicine shop in a Perak tin
town, opened by his father in 1879 —

242
00:16:35,225 --> 00:16:39,575
is still open for business,
147 years on: Japanese-owned,

243
00:16:39,655 --> 00:16:43,775
Singapore-headquartered,
and chaired by his grandson.

244
00:16:43,775 --> 00:16:46,900
The empire went; the shop endured.
